.TH NOW-PLAYING 1 "2026" "now-playing" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
now-playing \- media "now playing" state daemon (MPRIS + Chromecast fallback)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B setup.sh
[\fBinstall\fR|\fBservice\fR|\fBall\fR|\fBuninstall\fR|\fBcheck\fR|\fBtest\fR|\
\fBversion\fR]
.br
.B np-ctl
\fBplaypause\fR|\fBnext\fR|\fBprevious\fR
.br
.B now-playing
[\fBstatus\fR [\fB--json\fR] [\fB--follow\fR] [\fB--interval\fR \fISECS\fR] |\
 \fBshm-info\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B now-playing
is a systemd
.B --user
daemon that reads the current media state \(em the local MPRIS player via
playerctl, or a Chromecast via pychromecast when casting \(em and publishes it
as a shared-memory frame at
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/now-playing.frame .
A widget (for example a bar's media card) maps that page to render title,
artist, art, a progress scrubber, which transport controls are usable, and an
audio spectrum.
.PP
The daemon is the MODEL and a consumer is a VIEW. Every frame is complete, so a
consumer renders what it is handed and derives no playback facts of its own. Any
number of consumers may map the frame at once, each on its own clock, and the
daemon neither knows nor cares how many there are. The full specification is
.IR docs/contract.md ;
read it before writing a consumer.
.PP
.B np-ctl
sends a transport command (playpause, next, previous) to the daemon's control
FIFO
.RI ( $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/now-playing.ctl ),
which routes it to the live source. This works while casting, where a direct
playerctl call cannot reach the physical Chromecast. A short timeout guards a
dead daemon so a click handler never hangs.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install
Link np-ctl (+ man) into ~/.local.
.TP
.B service
Build the daemon venv (pychromecast) and enable its --user service.
.TP
.B all
install + service.
.TP
.B check
Command + deps present, venv + service up, and the frame actually LIVE (a
stronger claim than an active unit: it proves the daemon is publishing), as
.RB [ OK ]/[ FAIL ]
markers.
.TP
.B status
Print the current frame and exit.
.B --json
emits it machine-readably.
.B --follow
streams, emitting when something EVENTFUL changes (track, state, artwork,
capabilities); position deliberately does not count, since it moves every frame.
.B --interval
\fISECS\fR streams on a clock instead, position included. Exit status is 0 for a
live frame, 1 when there is none, 2 for usage, 3 for an unreadable layout
version.
.TP
.B shm-info
Print the segment path, layout version, geometry and liveness. Its real job is
version negotiation, so it reports a layout mismatch rather than failing on it.
.SH NOTES
playerctl is a system binary, not a venv dependency; the venv carries only
pychromecast (used only while casting; local playback needs none of it).
.PP
The frame is the ONLY published state. An earlier JSON file at
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/now-playing.state
has been removed: a second projection of the same facts is a second thing that
can drift. Use
.B status
for a shell-facing read of the frame.
